22 requests, before and after a one-line change to retrieval.
14 of them can only be answered from the documents; the rest have the
warehouse as an alternative route.

                                         healthy   degraded   alerts?
  HTTP errors                                  0          0   silent
  p95 latency                             8441ms     4430ms   silent
  tokens per request                        2196       1611   silent
  answers judged correct                    9/10       1/10   FIRES
  answers actually correct                 19/22       8/22   FIRES

Nothing failed. No request errored, no timeout was hit, no exception reached a
log, and the service returned 200 to everything. An uptime check would have
been green for the entire window, and an on-call engineer would have been
right to ignore it.

The system was, meanwhile, getting 11 more answers wrong out of 22.

And look at the two rows above it. p95 fell from 8441ms to 4430ms and
the average request got 27% cheaper, because a system that
cannot find anything stops looking sooner. An efficiency dashboard would
have shown this outage as a good week.

This is the shape of nearly every real degradation in this kind of system: an
index that rebuilt wrong, a prompt edit that shipped on Friday, a provider
silently routing to a different model revision. None of them are outages, all
of them are worse than outages, because an outage announces itself.

So the monitor has to be a quality signal, which means an eval running against
production traffic. Three ways to get one, cheapest first:

  refusal rate        free, already in your logs, and the earliest warning
                      you will get — a system that has stopped finding things
                      starts saying so before anyone complains
  judge on a sample   a few hundred graded answers a day is affordable and
                      catches what refusal rate misses: confident wrongness
  the golden set      run Chapter 21's cases against production hourly. It is
                      the only signal with certain labels, and the only one
                      that can page somebody at 3am without apology

Alert on the last one. Chart the first two. And set the threshold from §21.6's
interval, not from a round number — a five-point drop on forty cases is noise,
and paging somebody for noise is how a quality alert gets muted.
